      <purpose>To record the level of pregnancy-related precautions and care requirements applicable to an individual, as the basis for safe and appropriate clinical decision-making.</purpose>
      <use>Use to record the level of pregnancy-related precautions and care requirements applicable to an individual, as the basis for safe and appropriate clinical decision-making. The context may be reassessed and updated over time as clinical circumstances change.&#xD;
&#xD;
Each of the following mutually exclusive care states reflects a distinct circumstance in which a different level of pregnancy-related precautions is required:&#xD;
- 'Baseline precautions' - the default state applying when none of the other defined states apply, requiring routine pregnancy-related precautions because an unrecognised pregnancy cannot be excluded.&#xD;
- 'Planning pregnancy' - applies when the individual is actively planning or preparing for pregnancy, such as intentional discontinuation of contraception, preconception clinical optimisation, or commencement of fertility treatment, and elevated pregnancy-related precautions are required.&#xD;
- 'Pregnant' - applies when pregnancy has been clinically confirmed and pregnancy-specific precautions apply.&#xD;
- 'Post-pregnant' - applies during the recovery period following the end of a pregnancy, regardless of gestational age or outcome, during which 'Baseline precautions' apply in addition to any specific post-pregnancy care considerations.&#xD;
- 'Not applicable' - applies when pregnancy is physiologically impossible and pregnancy-related precautions are not required in the individual's current circumstances.&#xD;
&#xD;
Typical events that may trigger an update to the 'Pregnancy care context' include, but are not limited to:&#xD;
- Documentation of menarche: transition from 'Not applicable' to 'Baseline precautions'.&#xD;
- Decision to actively prepare for pregnancy, such as commencement of fertility treatment or intentional discontinuation of contraception: transition from 'Baseline precautions' to 'Planning pregnancy'.&#xD;
- Confirmation of pregnancy: transition from 'Baseline precautions' or 'Planning pregnancy' to 'Pregnant'.&#xD;
- Documented birth or pregnancy loss: transition from 'Pregnant' to 'Post-pregnant'.&#xD;
- Completion of the post-pregnant recovery period: transition from 'Post-pregnant' to 'Baseline precautions'.&#xD;
- Documentation of a formal diagnosis of menopause or a hysterectomy procedure: transition from 'Baseline precautions' to 'Not applicable'.&#xD;
&#xD;
This archetype is intended to be used alongside the OBSERVATION.pregnancy_assertion archetype. Together they supersede the traditional representation of a single 'Pregnancy status' concept by separating pregnancy assertion from pregnancy care context. Pregnancy assertion records whether an individual is believed to be pregnant or not at a specific point in time, while Pregnancy care context records the broader and more persistent clinical context and associated pregnancy-related precautions relevant to clinical decision-making.</use>
      <misuse>Not to record pregnancy confirmation or exclusion at a specific point in time. In particular, a clinician's determination that an individual is ‘not pregnant’ is only valid as a point-in-time clinical assertion and should not be used to represent an ongoing pregnancy care context. Use the OBSERVATION.pregnancy_assertion archetype for this purpose.&#xD;
&#xD;
Not to be used to record a diagnosis of, suspicion of, or level of uncertainty about pregnancy. Use the EVALUATION.problem_diagnosis for this purpose.&#xD;
&#xD;
Not to be used to record the summary information about a single pregnancy. Use the EVALUATION.pregnancy_summary for this purpose.</misuse>
